आदिकविः|आदिकवि
आदि + -कविः ‘the first poet’, an epithet of Brahmā and of Vālmīki;
 the former is so called because he first produced and promulgated the Vedas;
[<a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=AP&page=0328-1" target="_blank">Page0328-1</a>]
 (तेने ब्रह्म हृदा य आदिकवये मुह्यन्ति यत्सूरयः Bhāg. 1. 1. 1.)
 and the latter, because he was the first to show to others ‘the path of poets’
; when he beheld one of a pair of Krauñca birds being killed
 by a fowler, he cursed the wretch, and his grief unconsciously took the
 form of a verse (श्लोकत्वमापद्यत यस्य शोकः); he was subsequently
 told by Brahmā to compose the life of Rāma, and he thus gave to the
 world the first poem in Sanskrit, the Rāmāyaṇa; cf. U. 2 Viṣkambhaka.
<a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=AP&page=0327-2" target="_blank">PDF</a>
<a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=ap" target="_blank">Correction</a>

