जमदग्निः|जमदग्नि
जमदग्निः A Brāhmaṇa and descendant of Bhṛgu and father of Paraśurāma.
 [Jamadagni was the son of Ṛchika and Satyavatī. He was a pious sage,
 deeply engaged in study, and is said to have obtained entire possession
 of the Vedas. His wife was Reṇukā who bore him five sons. One day when
 she had gone out to bathe, she beheld a loving pair of Gandharvas (according
 to some Citraratha and his queen) sporting and playing in the water.
 The lovely sight made her feel envious of their pleasure, and she returned
 defiled by unworthy thoughts, ‘wetted but not purified by the stream’.
 Her husband, who was anger incarnate, seeing her shorn of the lustre of
 her sanctity, furiously scolded her, and ordered his sons, as they came
 in, to cut off her head. But the first four sons shrank from that cruel
 deed. It was only Paraśurāma, the youngest, that with characteristic
 obedience to his fatherʼs command, struck off her head with his axe. The
 deed pacified the fatherʼs anger, and he desired Paraśurāma to ask a
 boon. The kind-hearted son begged that his mother might be restored to
 life which the father readily granted.]
<a href="https://dub.sh/cslp?dict=AP&page=0727-2" target="_blank">PDF</a>
<a href="https://dub.sh/cslc?dict=ap" target="_blank">Correction</a>

